WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.430
JACK WEIXEL: My
name is Jack Weixel.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.070
And I manage global partnerships
for something in Android

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.617
that we call dedicated devices.

00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:14.700
I'll explain a little bit
more about what that is.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.242
But for now, you
can think about that

00:00:16.242 --> 00:00:19.110
as anything that's not
a smartphone, right?

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.110
So all the other use cases
that we'll talk about

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.112
falls into that category.

00:00:23.112 --> 00:00:25.570
I want to have a discussion
today about a couple of things.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.600
One-- I want to talk
about in Android,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.509
why we're placing a focus
on the enterprise use cases,

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:35.090
how we're addressing
those use cases,

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.630
what some of the
trends in industry

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.390
that we've seen regarding
the use of mobile operating

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.890
systems, and give
you some examples.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.270
And I'll show
customer use cases.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.080
That's my favorite part.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.420
And what I hope to do is walk
through, give some examples.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.620
We'll open up the
floor to questions

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.550
afterwards in case you have
some observations, questions,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.155
and obviously happy to
talk more about that.

00:00:57.155 --> 00:00:59.280
So I'm going to start out
with a couple of numbers.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.080
There's no Google
presentation that

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.840
can be done without numbers
to start things off with.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.270
And some of those numbers
you may already know.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.140
For example, there's 2.3
billion monthly active users

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.640
on Android--

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.890
every month 2.3
billion devices that

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.870
are used that are GMS devices.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.610
That's Google Mobile Services.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.990
You can think of that as
Play Services, everything

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:21.225
we use too in a mobile phone.

00:01:21.225 --> 00:01:23.100
I'm going to talk a
little bit more in detail

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.360
why this is important.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.210
But I wanted to talk first
off about what that scale is.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.000
Just to give you an indication,
no other operating system

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.182
has ever shipped 2.3
billion copies, right?

00:01:33.182 --> 00:01:35.140
And that's what we have--
monthly active users.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.000
That has a lot of
implications, so

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.713
what we have to do
for the ecosystem

00:01:38.713 --> 00:01:39.880
and how we need to build it.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.250
That also has implications about
what we're doing in enterprise.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.460
A key to that is Google Play.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.150
Obviously smartphones or
enterprise use cases--

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.720
enterprise devices require the
ability to install, manage, .

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.540
Update various applications on
a device and it is just massive.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.400
There's about 8r-- sorry,
94 billion apps were down--

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.730
94 billion apps were
downloaded last year.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:08.970
We see about 8 billion
installs every month.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.580
So if you think
about that-- in order

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.700
to manage that and ensure
security and ensure usability,

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.370
ensure consistency, we've
had to take all that

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.200
into consideration
as we're building out

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.180
to support this ecosystem.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.580
And we're creating that
ability to address this

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.580
that you could take
advantage of an enterprise--

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.230
even with your
enterprise use cases.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.040
Now one set of numbers
you may not be aware of

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.380
is the fact that there will
be about 300 million Android

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.580
phones that were shipped
for business use this year.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.340
That's just for business use.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.350
And we've broken
down because of how

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.890
we address the market into those
that are personally liable--

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.990
bring your own device--

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.150
or something that's
corporate owned--

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:53.730
227 million in personal liable
and 60 million in corporate

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:54.630
owned.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.480
Now 78% of devices--

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.580
78% of all the devices
shipped for business

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.600
are Android devices.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.015
So that's a big why as
to why we're building out

00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:08.640
what used to be considered kind
of a consumer operating system.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.090
And quite frankly, we went
to market initially focused

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.510
on consumers until we
saw a huge increase

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:16.900
in the business market.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.320
And since Lollipop,
we've spent an awful lot

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.040
of time building in capabilities
to address enterprise use

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:23.560
cases.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.290
Now the reason that
that's important

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.240
is prior to this
point, there were

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.930
lots of entrepreneurs creating
ways to manage, deploy, utilize

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.130
these devices.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.290
And they all ended
up being proprietary.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.810
And all ended up
fragmenting the marketplace.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.140
We recognized that there was
contrary to everything else we

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.460
did at Android, number one.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.668
It mentioned the bit about
compatibility and ensuring

00:03:43.668 --> 00:03:44.760
usability and so forth.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.260
If you have
fragmentation like this,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.340
that hurts everybody
across the ecosystem.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.310
So we decided to do,
starting in Lollipop,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.230
is really deliver
in the platform

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.990
the ability to manage devices
in a consistent fashion.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.572
We can take a look and see
Work Profile and Fully Managed.

00:03:57.572 --> 00:03:59.280
That's a scenario,
where, Fully Managed--

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.410
obviously the corporation
owns the device.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.510
You know, Work Profile
is where a corporation

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.250
owns a folder on your device.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.450
It's a separate user--

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.602
complete data separation
for those use cases.

00:04:08.602 --> 00:04:10.560
That's all I'm going to
say about Work Profile,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.610
because that's not
what this is about.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:16.410
But those are the two key pieces
to the management capability.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.329
When we got to
Marshmallow, that's

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.297
when we started
really focusing more

00:04:20.297 --> 00:04:21.839
on the enterprise
use cases that were

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.400
different from a smartphone.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.650
So we introduced Lock
Task mode, the ability

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.710
to set devices up more
easily in Kiosk mode.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:32.142
So you can deploy business
use cases-- something that

00:04:32.142 --> 00:04:33.600
might be unattended,
something that

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.700
will be different than
a smartphone scenario.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.140
And we've continued all through
these next couple of releases

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.830
more and more capabilities that
were addressed-- that simply

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.860
address the enterprise
use cases, including

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.030
being able to allow multiple
shifts for being able to log

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.140
into a device more easily.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.557
Instead of having to manage
all of that software in a very

00:04:54.557 --> 00:04:57.360
awkward fashion, we allow you
to sign in and sign out with P

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.725
with these various devices.

00:04:58.725 --> 00:05:00.600
That will apply to some
of the [? use case ?]

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.142
I'll talk about in
Rugged, as well as

00:05:02.142 --> 00:05:04.800
any of the public facing
multi-user sessions that we

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:05.982
talk about.

00:05:05.982 --> 00:05:07.440
The reason I'm
pointing all of this

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.450
out is if you're looking
at enterprise use case

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.742
and you're trying to figure
out how to [? put in ?] all

00:05:11.742 --> 00:05:13.500
the applications
that you want, you

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.395
don't have to build
that capability.

00:05:15.395 --> 00:05:17.020
We've got it addressed
in the platform.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.010
You can focus on what's
the value add instead

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.640
of just the base capability.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.168
Now underlying all of this-- you
know, the elephant in the room,

00:05:27.168 --> 00:05:28.710
by the way, with a
lot of discussions

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.585
I had early on when I
joined the Android team

00:05:30.585 --> 00:05:34.290
was security and this perception
that Android was less secure

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.600
than other operating systems.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.833
That's sort of been job
number one at Android--

00:05:38.833 --> 00:05:41.250
making sure that we address
that through security updates,

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.917
other capabilities
that we do that we'll

00:05:42.917 --> 00:05:44.380
touch on in just a second.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.200
But the pace of security
innovation has been massive--

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.280
in fact, so much so that Gartner
tells us that as of Oreo,

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.070
we actually surpassed
what they were

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.740
seeing in iOS,
which was typically

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.340
considered a more secure
operating system than Android.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.252
So the pace has been huge.

00:06:01.252 --> 00:06:03.460
It's not that others haven't
been innovating as well.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.070
We've just focused
so much on this.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.520
It's just insane.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.700
So if you're looking at
an enterprise devices,

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.980
there's no scenario
where security is more

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.560
important than in this piece.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.740
And that's been a
huge focus of ours.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.610
And why does this matter?

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:21.260
The reason all this matters
obviously is that a couple

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.740
of the statistics that we've
seen that have been presented

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:28.450
us from various analysts-- so
the fact that 78% of business

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.185
leaders-- just a
couple of numbers--

00:06:30.185 --> 00:06:30.810
this is Google.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.352
So I've got to give
you some numbers.

00:06:32.352 --> 00:06:34.420
78% of business leaders
expect their operations

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.105
to be a digital business
in the next few years.

00:06:37.105 --> 00:06:38.480
If you take a look
at the chart--

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.397
you already know this--
but if you take a look

00:06:40.397 --> 00:06:43.510
at the chart in terms of the
growth of these mobile devices,

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:49.480
it's insane in terms of
how quickly that's growing.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.102
The one that I always like to
point to is 90% of IT decision

00:06:52.102 --> 00:06:54.310
makers see Enterprise Mobility
as a critical function

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.833
for customer engagement,
experience, and so forth.

00:06:57.833 --> 00:06:59.500
I think the more
important factor that's

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.120
been [? in line, ?] this
is probably 10% of them

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.830
have to consider maybe
in a year or so what

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:04.315
they're going to do
at their next job,

00:07:04.315 --> 00:07:05.343
you know, quite frankly.

00:07:05.343 --> 00:07:06.760
It's gotten to the
point where you

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:08.860
need to be focused on
this type of thing.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.960
Or you need be focused
on something else--

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:12.460
[LAUGHS]

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.068
--that may be future employment.

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:17.860
Innovators across the
industry we're seeing

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:18.740
are turning to Android.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.740
Some of the things we'll
talk about, by the way,

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.380
are point of sale
and kiosk capability.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:27.580
And these are-- and
again, I know you all have

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:28.850
copies of this presentation.

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.517
You will receive
copies of presentation.

00:07:30.517 --> 00:07:33.970
So you can look up some of these
charts that I'll display here.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:35.230
But the growth in Kiosk--

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:38.200
if you've walked into an airport
lately, right, or fast food

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.420
store or just about
anywhere, you've

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.120
actually seen in terms
of growth, you know,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.010
what's been happening
in this marketplace.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:49.030
The point of sale
scenario is going

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:51.850
to be a huge trend-- is going
to undergo a huge transformation

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:53.100
over the next couple of years.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.690
It's already happening with
various restaurants you've

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:58.470
probably walked into--
various hospitals--

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.210
you know, hotels,
resorts, and so forth.

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:05.690
We're seeing increase in move
to Android through all of that.

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:07.780
So we need to be able to
support that and reduce

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.570
the fragmentation that have been
talked about earlier with some

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.487
of the knowledge worker
scenarios in this area

00:08:12.487 --> 00:08:14.780
before it actually happens.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.440
So just to give you an
idea, on the far left

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.480
are the two scenarios that
we started this whole journey

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:20.980
with.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:23.320
That's the knowledge worker
devices-- we refer to them--

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.230
handsets and tablets--
just accessing email,

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:28.090
corporate data, and so forth.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:31.030
What we're increasingly
focusing on-- my main objective

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.409
is all of these other
types of devices.

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:36.880
And we started with the rugged
devices for a simple reason.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:40.701
They most approached or looked
like your handsets, right?

00:08:40.701 --> 00:08:42.159
If you're familiar
with the Zebras,

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:44.472
the Honeywells, and so
forth, a lot of those devices

00:08:44.472 --> 00:08:45.430
look like your handset.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:46.222
They're ruggedized.

00:08:46.222 --> 00:08:48.550
They have other
capabilities and accessories

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.800
that people use
to do their jobs.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.210
But they look like a smartphone.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.735
And we used close cooperation
with these vendors

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:57.610
to be able to build into
the platform the way

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.710
to address other things,
such as, for example,

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:03.670
on a scanner in
the rugged device

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.590
if you're a point
of sale system,

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:07.870
there are other peripherals
you have to connect to.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:09.910
And the ability to manage
all of this-- again,

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:12.708
going back to the fact that
as you look at these use cases

00:09:12.708 --> 00:09:15.250
you want focus on software and
the value add and deliverable,

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:16.917
we need to be able
to address everything

00:09:16.917 --> 00:09:21.780
else underneath, from security
to management and so forth.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.500
So what we really want to
do at the end of the day

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.440
is allow our partners to
deliver to their customers

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.530
devices for any user no
matter what they're doing.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.880
And some of the particular
verticals or areas

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.870
that we see huge growth
in-- for example,

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.570
in retail, as well
as hospitality.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:42.480
You know, we'll go into a
couple of retail use cases

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:43.710
in a little bit.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.450
But you're here at Google Cloud.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.700
And when you think
of a customer journey

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.070
or if you think of your
operation of your store,

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.860
from the time something hits
your dock in the warehouse

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:55.410
[? or ?] the way it
travels through inventory

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.080
is delivered to user at the end
of the day, gets replenished.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.760
These are all ripe opportunities
for a Cloud Mobile scenario

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.030
to be able to improve processes,
insight into what's happening,

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.983
and customer experience
at the end of the day.

00:10:09.983 --> 00:10:12.400
Hospitality we'll talk a bit
a little about in more detail

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.450
as we get a little further.

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:15.033
But the idea of being
able to interact

00:10:15.033 --> 00:10:18.780
with your guests, your
customers in better ways

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:21.210
throughout whatever
the property might be

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.700
can be a huge competitive
advantage for you.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.730
And we're working
right now on how

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:30.570
to even go beyond what we've
done in the GMS scenario

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:34.800
to be able to improve
this to a great extent.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.075
Now it's not like
people aren't building.

00:10:37.075 --> 00:10:38.700
And some of you in
the room may already

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:42.090
be building some
applications or capabilities

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:43.960
to address these use cases.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.310
But what this started at was
in what we refer to as AOSP.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.860
That's the Android Open
System-- yeah, yeah, yeah--

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:53.150
Open Software Platform.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:54.720
It's-- sorry--

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.580
Android Open Source Platform.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:01.350
The difference when I refer
to GMS versus AOSP-- anybody

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.410
can do anything
they want with AOSP.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:04.410
You can build something.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:05.270
Of course, you have to.

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:07.140
We deliver a lot of
things in the platform.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.140
But you have to build your
own ways to manage the device,

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.880
deliver applications,
so on, and so forth.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.580
What we wanted to do with GMS--

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.400
with Play Service,
the objective is not

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.920
to get you to take
devices that have

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:23.760
Search on it, right, or Gmail.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:24.637
I mean, you could.

00:11:24.637 --> 00:11:26.220
There could be use
cases for something

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.483
like that-- certainly
Maps and some areas

00:11:28.483 --> 00:11:29.400
that we'll talk about.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:30.750
But really it's Play Services.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.250
When we go back to that
slide I talked about with all

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.250
those applications that
get downloaded and think

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:37.540
of having to update
those and address those--

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:41.040
if we leverage that to be able
to deploy these applications

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.620
and update applications
to your devices, that

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.900
makes your life a lot
easier, as well as being

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:48.630
able to utilize all the
APIs to secure and manage

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.070
those devices, as well--

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:53.430
everything we've built into
what we call GMS Core--

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:56.280
and that's the Play
Services underneath

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:59.200
to be able to support this.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:01.660
And some key pieces
within that story

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.090
are the ability to
enhance the security,

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.240
be able to deploy in a
very quick and easy way,

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.520
and utilize something
we call Manage Play.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.250
So enhanced security--
just to give you

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:15.370
an idea, when we talk about
these types of GMS devices,

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.700
all devices that have
Play Services on them

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.660
go through an approval process.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.050
So we understand
what the fingerprint

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:24.640
is, what the image looks like.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:29.200
And we can devise tests that
are done at machine speed,

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.350
constantly changing so
that we can tell even--

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.050
not just the fact that we
do all the scanning of apps

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:37.240
and make sure that
there isn't potentially

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.980
harmful applications being
delivered to your device--

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:42.380
we can tell afterwards
when a device is deployed,

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:43.990
whether it's been routed,
whether it's been tampered

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:45.935
with, whether it's still
secure, whether there

00:12:45.935 --> 00:12:46.810
are problems with it.

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:50.770
We can continue to
secure those devices even

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:52.540
after if the application's
been deployed,

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.550
the device is
deployed, and so forth.

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:57.370
Zero Touch Bulk Enrollment
is the capability

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:02.230
to, in a summary fashion,
be able to take a device out

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.340
of the box, turn it on, sign in.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.510
And everything
happens automatically.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:08.850
Your EMM gets attached to it.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.225
Your applications
get downloaded.

00:13:10.225 --> 00:13:13.120
It gets booted in the fashion
that you expect it to be done--

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.290
all of that without
user interaction.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:18.550
We make it very easy to
be able to deploy this.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:21.170
So again, when you're talking,
in an enterprise sense,

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:25.592
of the scale of thousands of
devices in multiple locations,

00:13:25.592 --> 00:13:27.800
the ability to take advantage
of this and deploy apps

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:29.800
is critical.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:33.520
Manage Google Play-- again, I
want to come back to the point

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.230
that when I talk about
Google Play a lot of times,

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:38.050
people think of
consumer apps, right?

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:40.220
You're going to download
Candy Crush or whatever--

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:40.720
[LAUGHS]

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:42.070
--onto a device.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:44.140
Manage Google Play
means the admin,

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.720
the company gets to
decide exactly what's

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:50.140
available on the device,
exactly what the application

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.250
store looks like,
as a matter of fact.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:53.650
You can open up to the world.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.270
You can publish
only private apps.

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:58.010
You can publish private
apps only to your devices.

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:00.440
You can allow a mix--
whatever you decide to do.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:02.920
And by the way, you can
make invisible to the user

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:07.060
and just deliver these
to the devices in a time

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:10.120
fashion that you choose and with
complete control over what's

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:11.000
happening.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:14.530
So all of this is an enterprise
focus, utilizing that platform,

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:17.410
but doing it in such a way
that you have complete control.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.740
This is not, you know, an open
to Google, open to the world

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:23.120
type of scenario.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:28.640
And by the way, one of the
things I always get asked about

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:30.830
is what is Google
looking at on my devices.

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:34.340
A key thing with Manage Google
Play is it's a device account.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:36.030
It's not a user account.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.600
So we alleviate the concern
over people wondering

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:43.160
what's made available,
what's viewed, what's seen,

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:45.330
and so forth.

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:47.150
Now because of all
this work that we've

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:50.360
done, when we take a look
at the percentage of devices

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.000
that are free of
potentially harmful apps--

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.970
99.99% of devices.

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.880
And the two bars we have here--

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:02.540
the green one is one that is
not using Google Play only

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:03.600
to deploy apps.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.960
You know, the blue one
is using Google Play only

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:07.470
to go ahead and do that.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:10.603
The alternative, by the way, to
do this is to side load an app.

00:15:10.603 --> 00:15:12.770
To side load an app, you
have to open up your device

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.320
to install applications
from unknown sources

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:18.800
and utilize something called
a device administrator or EPI,

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.785
which we're
deprecating, by the way.

00:15:20.785 --> 00:15:21.785
This opens up security--

00:15:21.785 --> 00:15:22.285
[LAUGHS]

00:15:22.285 --> 00:15:24.260
--holes that did not
exist in Android.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:25.850
And going back to what I
talked about earlier, that

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:27.558
was a key reason we
had to close that off

00:15:27.558 --> 00:15:29.878
and deliver an alternative
so that you can actually

00:15:29.878 --> 00:15:30.920
have this kind of result.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:32.750
And I'll tell you what--

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:35.000
the green-- every-- because
of all the capabilities

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.110
that we built into Android
to improve security,

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:39.760
you see even the green
going down dramatically.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.690
But its worst point--

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:44.990
5.3% of devices.

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:46.670
Yeah, I imagine
[? all ?] your desktops,

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:50.950
you'd be pretty happy
with potentially only 5.3%

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:51.880
of those devices.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:53.130
But we're not happy with that.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:54.957
We want 0.01%.

00:15:54.957 --> 00:15:56.790
And that's what we get
at the end of the day

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.270
with taking advantage of
this kind of platform.

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:00.610
So it's not just making it easy.

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:01.860
It's not making it consistent.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:05.390
It's actually making
it rock solid secure.

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:06.730
And I've talked about rugged.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:09.260
That was the first group
to actually engage with us

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:11.000
from the enterprise scenario.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:12.290
We've seen a huge trend.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:15.620
These are reported to us by
partners and analysts in terms

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:19.050
of where the market is going.

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:20.030
And you think about it.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:22.060
These types of devices-- this
comes from an industry, where

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:24.730
people want to lock things off
from exposure to the internet,

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:27.070
because they want to
secure the device, right?

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:28.570
So in this marketplace
when people

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:32.410
are accepting Android
and moving to Android,

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:35.325
we're winning the game, because
we're delivering results

00:16:35.325 --> 00:16:38.330
that we expect to do.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.380
So let me talk about
a couple of things.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.160
I want to start--

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:43.970
by the way, there's
a couple of videos.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:45.970
There's only a couple
that I'll actually play.

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:51.410
But these videos-- you can go to
Android.com/Enterprise and take

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.450
a look and see what they
actually use cases are.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:54.920
And I encourage you to
do so, because they're

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.378
pretty interesting--
the innovation

00:16:56.378 --> 00:16:58.700
and what people are
doing in these scenarios.

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:02.030
But an example of what we're
talking about here-- and this

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:02.690
is Walmart.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:04.280
And they're using Zebra devices.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:08.589
And I believe SAP is
their Cloud application

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:10.190
that they're delivering.

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:12.920
They've-- obviously you're
familiar with Walmart.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:14.599
Think about two
million employees.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:17.240
And what they are faced with
a scenario where they wanted

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.550
to be able to give one
device to their associates

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:21.380
to do a number of
different things.

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:22.994
And they will look up prices.

00:17:22.994 --> 00:17:24.619
They will actually
scan and take a look

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:25.819
at inventory, what's available.

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:27.589
And in the same app--
in the same device,

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:28.460
they'll be able to say, OK.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:29.840
Well, I'm out of this product.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:31.213
When's my delivery date, right?

00:17:31.213 --> 00:17:32.630
They can see that
on their device.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.910
They can take a look and
see when they have ordered.

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:40.910
They can start to make decisions
based on what trends have been,

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:43.550
you know, not just a device, but
from the corporate standpoint

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:47.240
by connecting this to
obviously a Cloud App to be

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:49.490
able to make better decisions
about when they're going

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:52.115
to order, what they're going to
do improving customer journeys,

00:17:52.115 --> 00:17:52.860
and so forth.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.430
This is the type of
back to front scenario

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:57.523
that we've seen repeatedly
in this industry.

00:17:57.523 --> 00:17:59.190
And this is a really
good example of it.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:01.085
What I love about
this, though, is

00:18:01.085 --> 00:18:03.710
that I've seen this come up in
a number of different use cases,

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:05.360
where someone
unsolicited will say,

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:07.910
you know, it works just
like my smartphone.

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:09.710
And if you think
about it, when you're

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:13.250
talking about doing a
transformational change--

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:14.667
transforming any
business process,

00:18:14.667 --> 00:18:17.000
getting somebody to do something
a little bit different,

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.125
introducing a new product,
the most difficult thing

00:18:19.125 --> 00:18:20.750
is getting somebody
to accept it and be

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.250
able to learn what
to do differently

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:23.820
than they had been before.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.500
Taking advantage of a platform
where you get that reaction--

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:28.208
it's just like my
smartphone-- that makes

00:18:28.208 --> 00:18:29.420
your job a whole lot easier.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:30.920
And that's one of
the reasons people

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:33.500
will turn to Android to
go ahead and do this.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:36.170
So from this perspective, it's
a great use case to take a look

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.097
and see how people are
using these devices to be

00:18:39.097 --> 00:18:41.180
able to get a 360 degree
view of everything that's

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:44.370
happening in the enterprise.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:51.260
So what we've done to be able to
support all of these activities

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:54.170
is to be able to introduce
a couple of key programs

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:56.240
into the scenario.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:58.820
One is what we call Android
Enterprise Recommended.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:01.040
We were constantly
asked, what do you

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:04.760
recommend for an enterprise to
use-- what types of devices.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.100
And this applies,
as we eventually

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:09.980
realized, not just to a
particular phone that you're

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:12.020
going to use for email,
but across the board

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:13.730
to rugged devices, to EMMs--

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:15.650
everything that's
part of the ecosystem.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:18.770
And we built an Android
Enterprise Recommended program

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:21.650
to even ensure that you're
going to have conference

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:23.240
as a customer in
selecting a device,

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:26.335
consistency in deployment, and
making sure that it's current

00:19:26.335 --> 00:19:26.960
and up to date.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:31.700
We have requirements in terms
of how quickly app devices need

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:33.710
to be upgraded--

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:37.843
security updates delivered,
and so forth, so as well

00:19:37.843 --> 00:19:40.010
as making sure that it can
be delivered and deployed

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:42.820
with zero touch in all the
ways that we expect to do.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.320
It's going to look and feel the
same way when it's deployed.

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:49.620
Obviously all these capabilities
are important from an Android

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:50.690
Enterprise Recommended.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:52.340
But again, coming
back to anything

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.963
that you might be doing in
your particular business,

00:19:55.963 --> 00:19:57.380
you can be comfortable
in the fact

00:19:57.380 --> 00:19:59.090
that we've built
the infrastructure

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:00.715
and the capability,
particularly if you

00:20:00.715 --> 00:20:03.200
choose some of these partners,
to be able to deploy things

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:04.940
in a way that makes
a lot of sense

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:07.140
and you can be very
comfortable with.

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:10.180
So just to get a quick view
from the rugged perspective,

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:11.930
by the way, we started
out with five OEMs.

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:12.870
That quickly expanded.

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:13.850
There's still a few more--

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:14.180
[LAUGHS]

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:15.630
--that are waiting
to be updated.

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:17.790
So it's been a huge uptake.

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:19.800
And one of the
simple reasons are

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:21.770
is people are starting
now to make choices

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:24.080
on particular devices based
on whether they're Android

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.120
Enterprise Recommended or not.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.340
Customers always want to
be comfortable with what

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:29.660
they're buying.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:30.220
OK.

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:32.720
So that's a little bit of the
background on what we're doing

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:33.660
and why we're doing it.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:35.827
But let's talk about some
of these Android Dedicated

00:20:35.827 --> 00:20:36.920
Devices.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.000
And the rugged device
we already talked about.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.510
But what we're doing
is now seeing--

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:45.620
is we've talked about more
activity in point of sale,

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:48.210
embedded devices,
kiosk scenarios.

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:52.160
And when you think about
this and all the capabilities

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:54.140
that Google will bring,
not just in terms

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:56.375
of device in your
scenario, one thing

00:20:56.375 --> 00:20:58.250
that I didn't mention
a couple of slides back

00:20:58.250 --> 00:20:59.240
that I'll bring
I'll bring up now

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.740
as we talk about this, because
when you take a look at this,

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:06.110
when kiosk and point of sale,
retail is a great vertical.

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:09.280
And when you think back
to you what you do,

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.030
particularly if you're
in a retail segment

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:13.438
in all aspects of
your business, one

00:21:13.438 --> 00:21:15.230
of the things I mentioned
a few slides back

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:16.970
is other Google technologies.

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:19.790
You know, one key is
augmented reality.

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:23.270
So imagine, if you
will, a scenario

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:24.710
where you could take a device--

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:27.830
and you are in a warehouse-- to
be able to walk through and use

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:30.860
augmented reality for anyone
that wants to pick something

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:33.290
to be able to go
immediately to that shelf

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:36.140
or to be able to
walk to a scenario

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:39.950
where you're fixing a component
and to be able to have overlaid

00:21:39.950 --> 00:21:43.370
not only instructions on what
to fix-- maybe it's actually

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:47.300
because you've already captured
information about which device

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:50.270
is failing, you know, what
needs to be done, and so forth.

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:53.193
The device can actually
lead you to understand

00:21:53.193 --> 00:21:55.610
what you need to do and which
device needs to be replaced,

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:57.660
all of which, by the
way, exists right now.

00:21:57.660 --> 00:21:59.577
And those are use cases
people are delivering.

00:21:59.577 --> 00:22:04.600
So you can build that type of
capability into your scenario.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:07.330
So how are some of the
dedicated devices being used?

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:09.430
And in full disclosure,
this particular slide

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:10.550
is actually an example.

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:12.383
But I stole it from
the Chrome guys,

00:22:12.383 --> 00:22:14.300
because this was actually
a Chrome deployment.

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:15.945
You can do the same
thing in Android.

00:22:15.945 --> 00:22:18.070
What you may not be aware
of is there are proximity

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:19.850
APIs in all these devices.

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:23.230
So if you have an application--
maybe you're a retailer.

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.550
And you have an application--
might be a loyalty app.

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:27.370
And someone has
actually opted in

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:32.790
to be able to get offers
or notices from you.

00:22:32.790 --> 00:22:35.200
As they walk by a
particular display that

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:37.250
could be geared to be
able to deliver to them

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:39.085
either content offers
and so forth that

00:22:39.085 --> 00:22:41.060
would apply to that individual.

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:43.420
So these are the types
of things that actually,

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:45.460
if it's done in
the right way, can

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:49.060
be really compelling
in a user experience.

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:52.770
So Chronopost is
a delivery company

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:56.510
based in Paris similar to
FedEx, UPS, and so forth.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:58.500
And a while back,
they decided that they

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.240
needed to revamp their fleet.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:03.540
They're delivering about
a million packages a day.

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:05.910
And they saw a lot
of inefficiencies

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:07.830
throughout the whole
scenario, from being

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:10.650
able to track what devices
are picked up to be scheduling

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:13.050
routes and so forth.

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:15.980
What they opted to do is they
worked with a partner, Zebra,

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:18.990
and one of our EMM partners
to be able to design a system,

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:22.170
whereby they would be
able to have a driver--

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.700
number one, when he
walks in in the morning

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.460
to be able to pick a
device up and drop it

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:29.280
into his truck or her truck.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.230
And it's got the map laid out.

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.390
It's got all his
delivery schedule already

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:34.020
set up for him.

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.150
He just has to follow, you
know, what the device is

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:36.970
telling him to do.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:40.607
And that's all driven by an
application that understands,

00:23:40.607 --> 00:23:41.940
you know, what are the packages.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:42.910
What are the locations?

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:45.070
What is traffic in the area--

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:50.020
you know, other disruptions
to a particular route?

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:53.042
And they use Google Maps to
do a lot of this in addition

00:23:53.042 --> 00:23:54.500
to what they're
doing on the device

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:55.960
in their own application.

00:23:55.960 --> 00:23:57.480
And if you're not
aware, there are

00:23:57.480 --> 00:24:01.680
APIs that you can build other
capabilities into Google Maps.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:04.080
So for example, if
you're a delivery driver,

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:05.910
maybe you have a
particular address.

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:08.470
But the loading dock happens
to be around the corner

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:10.330
on another physical address.

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:12.750
Or it might-- and it might
be a little bit difficult

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:14.250
to get to have to
pick the scenario.

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.780
You can build that
into the app so

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:19.080
that when the driver is
approaching his destination,

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:21.323
that driver is going
to the correct area,

00:24:21.323 --> 00:24:22.740
navigating with
the right street--

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:25.170
makes it much more
efficient capability

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:27.220
to be able to deliver
those devices.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:34.890
So it's been a huge
success in their deployment

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:37.200
and not only in terms
of helping the drivers,

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.540
but is there capturing
all the information

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:40.660
about what's happening.

00:24:40.660 --> 00:24:42.180
What are the routes?

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.460
What are the failures in
the trucks and so forth?

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:47.790
There's a load of information
that produce analytics

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.378
that they can use to
dictate and determine

00:24:50.378 --> 00:24:52.170
how they want to make
changes and what they

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:57.770
want to deploy in going forward

00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:00.350
Marks &amp; Spencer you may be
familiar with-- a big retailer

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:01.890
in the UK.

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:04.790
They worked with a
partner, Honeywell, to say,

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:05.380
you know what?

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:07.250
We want to build a
device in the future.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.580
What the associates were
doing-- and they describe

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:12.480
to be five or six devices.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:13.800
One is to check a price.

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:15.800
You know, one is to see
what's in the inventory.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:19.910
One is to understand a
customer's order status,

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:21.390
you know, so on, and so forth.

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:23.293
They decided that they
would take one device.

00:25:23.293 --> 00:25:25.460
And instead of having all
of these different devices

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:27.002
do things, they
would have one device

00:25:27.002 --> 00:25:29.930
that has apps to run all
these different capabilities.

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:32.180
They spent a lot of
time actually working

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:34.190
with their associates
to understand

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:38.980
how they do their daily
job, what's problems,

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:41.150
what's headaches, and so forth.

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:43.670
And they rolled
this about a year

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:45.990
ago out to their customers.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:50.870
And it's really interesting
when you listen to the video.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:53.300
The woman that was actually
heading this up says this is--

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:55.040
I don't know how long she
had been at Marks &amp; Spencer--

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.260
but this is probably the
most successful rollout

00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:58.697
that they ever had.

00:25:58.697 --> 00:26:00.530
You listen to some of
the associates talking

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:03.290
about how great it is to have
this device in their pocket

00:26:03.290 --> 00:26:05.020
that makes their lives
that much easier.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:06.770
What it results in at
the end of the day--

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:09.522
they have statistics that
they talk about in the video--

00:26:09.522 --> 00:26:11.480
were improved customer
satisfaction, as well as

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:13.188
employee satisfaction
to a market degree.

00:26:13.188 --> 00:26:16.850
And it all can be traced to when
they rolled this program out.

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:18.500
So there's hard
data in terms of how

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.210
this makes an actual
difference, not

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:23.720
just to your customers at the
end of day, but the employees,

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:25.972
as well.

00:26:25.972 --> 00:26:28.180
Most of you are probably
familiar with Pitney Bowes--

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:30.130
a postage meter company.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:32.440
Pitney Bowes a while back
decided that, you know what?

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.760
We need to change our
focus a little bit.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:39.460
We need to incorporate some of
the new mobile capabilities.

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:43.063
We need to deliver things to our
customers in a different way.

00:26:43.063 --> 00:26:44.980
We want to deliver
capabilities that they just

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.173
don't have today.

00:26:46.173 --> 00:26:47.840
So what they did is
they worked with us.

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:52.450
They built a postage meter that
has an embedded 7-inch tablet

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:53.320
in it.

00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:56.360
And what they deliver
to their users--

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:58.610
I'm not sure if you're
familiar with their background.

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:01.230
But all these are regulated.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:04.420
So if you find a postage
meter in a customer account,

00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:05.640
it's not-- nobody owns it.

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:07.300
That's all managed
by Pitney Bowes.

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:09.460
But what they do is
they deliver a web store

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:12.430
so that the customers can
buy certain services go well

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:14.693
beyond weighing a package
and making a delivery.

00:27:14.693 --> 00:27:16.360
You know, part of
that is an application

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:19.430
that would do the calculation
for the customer to say,

00:27:19.430 --> 00:27:19.930
listen.

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:22.030
Here's a package
going to x location.

00:27:22.030 --> 00:27:24.280
It's y weight.

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:25.120
Use FedEx.

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:28.960
Or use UPS or if they choose
the most efficient or least

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:31.073
expensive way to go and
deliver that package.

00:27:31.073 --> 00:27:32.740
There are other
capabilities that people

00:27:32.740 --> 00:27:35.830
would use on a daily basis that
could be used on this device,

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:38.170
ranging from things like
inventory, time sheets,

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:39.290
and so forth.

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:41.240
So a device that you
think of as very simple--

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:42.073
you weigh a package.

00:27:42.073 --> 00:27:44.980
You ship it-- can actually
be changed and used

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:47.770
to be able to deliver
capabilities to end users

00:27:47.770 --> 00:27:52.670
that previously, you
wouldn't be able to deliver.

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:55.970
So MiCab is a company that's
based in the Philippines.

00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:58.690
And they have a ride
sharing application.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:02.260
They're taking-- I'm sure a
lot of people in this room

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:07.780
are familiar with Lyft or Uber
and what you get with your app

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:10.570
to be able to rate, understand
who's coming to get you,

00:28:10.570 --> 00:28:12.340
what the reality is.

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:14.380
So imagine taking that
type of capability.

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:16.005
And what they did
was they applied that

00:28:16.005 --> 00:28:17.450
to the taxicab industry.

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:19.780
So it's all taxi cabs as
opposed to Uber or Lyft.

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:22.100
But what they have is you
can think of three devices.

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:26.710
One is the driver's device that
sits in the front of the cab.

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:30.730
And it's generally a
smartphone type of form factor

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:34.780
that allows them to
get new customers,

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:37.370
understand what the route is
to the customer destination,

00:28:37.370 --> 00:28:38.470
and so forth.

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:41.770
A device that's more tablet
sized is in the back seat.

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:44.900
And that's used to do everything
from display entertainment.

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:46.810
They have some ads capabilities.

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:50.113
They allow the customer
to pay on that device.

00:28:50.113 --> 00:28:52.030
And of course, that's
all connected via an app

00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:53.330
to the customer's device.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.150
So all of this is tied together.

00:28:55.150 --> 00:29:00.090
And when you think about this,
it sounds kind of simple.

00:29:00.090 --> 00:29:02.810
And it's a taxicab app.

00:29:02.810 --> 00:29:06.220
It allows you to understand that
who your driver is going to be.

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:07.720
And you're getting
in the right cab.

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:10.618
It's going to be well
maintained and so forth.

00:29:10.618 --> 00:29:11.410
But think about it.

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:14.740
They're using these devices
to make their payment, right?

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:16.892
So they have to be
absolutely secure--

00:29:16.892 --> 00:29:18.850
it's not just a matter
of making it available--

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:19.930
absolutely secure.

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:22.278
And it has to work, because
if it's not working,

00:29:22.278 --> 00:29:23.320
they don't take payments.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:25.010
And everything falls apart.

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:27.170
So this is kind of
an innovated way

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:29.380
of taking what we've
learned in other areas,

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:32.980
applying it to an existing
business to kind of transform

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:36.010
and make it more up to date in
terms of what customers expect.

00:29:36.010 --> 00:29:38.380
And I want to play
a short clip here

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:40.600
in terms of what the
customer is looking for

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:43.480
and what they saw is
advantages about the platform

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:45.310
as was the partners
they were working with.

00:29:45.310 --> 00:29:48.068
The key piece that they
wanted to talk about--

00:29:48.068 --> 00:29:49.485
and again, this
is on the website.

00:29:49.485 --> 00:29:52.210
So you can go and listen to
them tell it in their words.

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:56.000
But they've got thousands of
devices that are deployed,

00:29:56.000 --> 00:29:56.500
you know.

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:59.410
And there's new cabs
coming online and so forth.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:01.108
They have to do this remotely.

00:30:01.108 --> 00:30:02.650
They have to be able
to do it easily.

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:04.317
They have to do in
an automated fashion.

00:30:04.317 --> 00:30:07.900
They can't send someone out to
all these different locations

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:11.260
to be able to set it up
and deploy the devices.

00:30:11.260 --> 00:30:13.840
All of this is managed by
the platform that's built

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:16.060
into Android Play Services.

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:17.700
And of course, as I
mentioned, security

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:19.220
is absolutely critical.

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:23.980
So they talk a little bit in
detail in the presentation

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:28.730
as to why they chose that
and what the impact was.

00:30:28.730 --> 00:30:34.320
So Yumpingo is-- anybody
here from the UK, by the way?

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:34.820
No?

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:35.320
[LAUGHS]

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:35.860
OK.

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:40.490
Yumpingo is a company based in
London that goes to restaurants

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:43.880
and delivers a service,
whereby at the end of a meal,

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.730
they deliver a folder
along with your bill that

00:30:46.730 --> 00:30:49.430
includes a device that
looks like a 5 and 1/2 inch

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:50.060
smartphone.

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:51.000
It's not a smartphone.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:52.640
It's actually running one app.

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:55.460
And that app is to be able
to rate your experience--

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:58.760
to be able to rate, you know,
how the server was, cleanliness

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:01.820
of the restaurant, how the
food was, the different dishes,

00:31:01.820 --> 00:31:02.430
and so forth.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:04.370
Of course, it's
got all the detail,

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:07.040
because it's tied to the
order that was delivered.

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:11.720
And they've had unbelievable
response from customers

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:12.770
in terms of rating this.

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:15.830
Everybody seems to want to
go ahead and rate the app.

00:31:15.830 --> 00:31:18.810
And what's important about
it is not only can they get--

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:24.410
it's kind of TripAdvisor type
scenario or Yelp and so forth--

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:25.970
ratings of that
particular restaurant

00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:26.887
that they can publish.

00:31:26.887 --> 00:31:28.250
They can advertise and so forth.

00:31:28.250 --> 00:31:31.100
But they get really
critical information

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:35.150
about what's happening across
all of their enterprise.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:37.370
It could be a small
restaurant chain

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:39.080
of three different locations.

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:42.110
But if one dish is
great at two of them

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:46.580
and really lousy at one or
service is the same way,

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:48.202
it's important to
know that, right?

00:31:48.202 --> 00:31:50.160
It's important to get
that kind of information.

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:51.577
There's loads of
other more detail

00:31:51.577 --> 00:31:53.970
that you can get out of
this type of application.

00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:55.637
And when you think
about that and you're

00:31:55.637 --> 00:31:57.980
thinking of various
applications that

00:31:57.980 --> 00:31:59.540
matter to your
particular industry,

00:31:59.540 --> 00:32:02.300
that type of analytical
approach and data gathering

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:05.170
would be critical in terms
of what you could actually

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:08.030
deliver.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:09.420
So as far as use
cases, I'm going

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:10.080
to close with the last one.

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:11.490
This is actually my favorite.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:14.150
And you know this is--

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:15.720
I had to have it rhyme, right?

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:17.870
So it's inspiration,
innovation, and celebration

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:18.970
the end of the day.

00:32:18.970 --> 00:32:20.730
But the point I
want to make here

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:24.490
is if you're looking
at enterprise use cases

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:26.240
and you're looking at
what you want to do,

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:29.000
obviously there's a scenario
that you would think

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:31.610
can be improved or
that you can think

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:34.880
can be deliver capabilities
that's not previously done.

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:37.970
The important thing is to focus
on what is that inspiration.

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:39.950
What is that key use
case, because you

00:32:39.950 --> 00:32:42.390
can innovate using the platform
to do some amazing things?

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:44.880
You're going to celebrate
at the end of the day.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:47.540
But hopefully, the audio is
going to work on this next one,

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:50.210
because I want to lead
into it with an example

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:51.165
of that inspiration.

00:32:51.165 --> 00:32:51.665
So--

00:32:55.276 --> 00:32:56.200
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:57.330
Samantha and her friend--

00:32:57.330 --> 00:32:57.830
[LAUGHS]

00:32:57.830 --> 00:32:59.500
--I'll give the voiceover here--

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:03.065
traveled to Brazil a
while ago on vacation.

00:33:03.065 --> 00:33:04.690
And the interesting
thing about Brazil,

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:06.107
if you know anything
about Brazil,

00:33:06.107 --> 00:33:07.958
is nobody speaks English there.

00:33:07.958 --> 00:33:08.500
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:09.930
JACK WEIXEL: Nobody speaks
anything but Portuguese

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:10.763
as a matter of fact.

00:33:10.763 --> 00:33:12.088
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:12.088 --> 00:33:12.823
[LAUGHS]

00:33:12.823 --> 00:33:13.490
JACK WEIXEL: OK.

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:18.730
So a few people maybe, but what
actually ended up happening

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:20.620
is Samantha and her
friend walked in

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:23.917
to various restaurants and
would try to order and asked,

00:33:23.917 --> 00:33:25.000
does anyone speak English?

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:27.067
And the answer, of
course, was always no.

00:33:27.067 --> 00:33:28.900
And of course, not being
able to communicate

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:31.220
means that you can't
order anything.

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.610
So at the end of the day,
what ended up happening is

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:36.400
they walked out
of 11 restaurants

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:39.130
over the course of
the time and were not

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:40.570
able to order in any of them.

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:41.970
They return to the States.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:43.720
And by this time, they
were really hungry.

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:45.290
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:45.290 --> 00:33:47.570
So the first thing they
do is go to a restaurant

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:48.820
to be able to order something.

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:50.237
And the waiter
brings them a menu,

00:33:50.237 --> 00:33:53.500
you know, takes their order,
understands what they want,

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:55.990
walks up to the cash register,
the point of sale device,

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:57.320
enters it in.

00:33:57.320 --> 00:34:01.120
And at that point in time,
the inspiration strikes.

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:03.760
And so Samantha says to
her friend, you know,

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:07.300
wouldn't it be great if I
had a mobile tablet instead

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:09.550
of a paper tablet, right?

00:34:09.550 --> 00:34:12.440
And I'd be able to understand
what I want and order it.

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:15.340
And by the way, maybe
I speak English.

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:16.940
Maybe I'm in Brazil.

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:19.768
But I would be able to order in
English so that I understand.

00:34:19.768 --> 00:34:21.310
What ends up happening
in the kitchen

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:23.560
is they get the order in
whatever their local language

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:24.310
is.

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:26.139
And they'd be able
to prepare the food,

00:34:26.139 --> 00:34:27.159
deliver it to the table.

00:34:27.159 --> 00:34:28.485
And everything is good.

00:34:28.485 --> 00:34:29.920
You know, everything
is wonderful.

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:32.392
So taking advantage of this
type of mobile application

00:34:32.392 --> 00:34:33.850
can deliver a
scenario where you've

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:37.000
got less mistakes, faster
service, efficient staff,

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:38.830
and of course,
increase your revenue.

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:40.900
And from that
standpoint, that's--

00:34:40.900 --> 00:34:42.310
Urban Translations was born.

00:34:42.310 --> 00:34:43.705
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:43.705 --> 00:34:46.929
[LAUGHS]

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:50.460
I've seen this once or twice.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:53.730
So what actually happens-- this
was a scenario of a restaurant.

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:55.560
Urban Translations
has done a lot of work

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:58.470
not only in restaurants,
but hotels and resorts

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:00.480
to be able to deliver
capabilities such

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:02.970
that it's not just
ordering food, right?

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:04.710
Imagine when you
walk into a hotel.

00:35:04.710 --> 00:35:07.127
And in your room, there's a
particular device that ends up

00:35:07.127 --> 00:35:08.670
being your digital concierge.

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:11.010
And maybe you're ordering
food in a particular location.

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:13.650
Maybe you're asking
for certain services.

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:17.025
Maybe you want to find
out locations to go to.

00:35:17.025 --> 00:35:19.290
To be able to communicate
in your own language

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:21.540
and to be able to
have that seamless

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:24.980
and allow us to interact, it
obviously helps the customer.

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:27.520
But it helps the staff as well.

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:29.430
And even if it's
not a scenario where

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:32.670
